>> title: Tuna Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: tuna, mayonnaise, no-cook, sandwich filling, make-ahead
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: tuna fish salad, tuna mayo, tuna fish, deli tuna salad, tuna sandwich filling
>> pairs-with: mayonnaise, white-sandwich-bread, sour-dill-pickles
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr 15 min
>> step.1: drain and flake the tuna
>> step.2: whisk the dressing
>> step.3: fold in the crunch
>> step.4: chill 1 hr

Drain @oil-packed tuna{10%oz}(285 g; two tins) hard against the lid, then flake it into a bowl with a fork, leaving some pieces the size of a thumbnail. Oil-packed rather than water-packed: the water-packed tin has already given its flavour to the water you are pouring away, and no amount of mayonnaise puts it back.

Whisk @mayonnaise{1/3%cup}(80 mL), @lemon juice{2%tsp}, @Dijon mustard{1%tsp}, @kosher salt{1/4%tsp} and @black pepper{1/4%tsp} smooth. Start with less mayonnaise than you expect to use — a good tuna salad is bound, not swimming, and the tin's own oil is already doing some of the work.

Fold @&(~1)dressing{} into @&(~2)flaked tuna{} with @celery{2%ribs}(diced small), @red onion{2%Tbs}(diced fine) and @flat-leaf parsley{2%Tbs}(chopped). Crunch is the whole architecture here; without the celery it is a paste.

Chill @&(~1)tuna salad{} ~chill{1%hr}. On rye toast with a slice of tomato, or in a scoop on a plate of lettuce, which is how the case sells it by the half pound.
